---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const today = '2026-07-10T00:00:00+00:00';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test is a free, browser-based tool that measures your typing as net words per minute, with your accuracy and keystrokes per hour. It offers four durations and lets you download a dated PDF report for job applications. It needs no signup and sends nothing to a server.' },
  { q: 'How is typing speed measured in WPM?', a: 'A word in WPM is standardized to five characters, including spaces, so scores stay comparable. Gross WPM is characters divided by five, then by minutes. Net WPM subtracts uncorrected errors first. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows net WPM as your main score, because it reflects the output you can actually use.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between gross WPM and net WPM?', a: 'Gross WPM ignores mistakes and counts every character. Net WPM subtracts your uncorrected errors before the sum, so errors lower it. For example, 300 characters in a minute is 60 gross WPM, but a handful of errors might make it 58 net. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test leads with net WPM as the honest figure.' },
  { q: 'What is a good typing speed?', a: 'The average is about 52 WPM, from a study of 168,000 typists, with most people between 30 and 60 and the fastest at 120. So around 50 is average, the 60s and 70s are quick, and 90 or more is exceptional. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows where your result lands.' },
  { q: 'What typing speed should I put on a resume?', a: 'List your net WPM with your accuracy, since one without the other means little. Many office roles are comfortable with 50 to 60 WPM at 95 percent accuracy or better, and data-entry roles often want more. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test gives you a dated PDF of net WPM and accuracy you can attach as proof.' },
  { q: 'How do I convert WPM to keystrokes per hour?', a: 'Multiply net WPM by 300, because a word is five characters and an hour is sixty minutes. So 40 net WPM is 12,000 KPH, and 60 is 18,000 KPH. Many data-entry job ads list requirements in KPH. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows your KPH next to your WPM so you can copy it straight across.' },
  { q: 'Why does accuracy matter as much as speed?', a: 'Because every error has to be found and fixed, which costs more than the moment it took to type. Net WPM subtracts uncorrected errors, so a clean 55 beats a messy 70. Aim for 95 percent accuracy first and speed will follow. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows accuracy live so you can feel the trade-off.' },
  { q: 'How can I type faster?', a: 'Keep your eyes on the screen rather than the keyboard, and practice 15 to 20 minutes a day, drilling the letters that trip you up. Research found fast typists overlap keystrokes and that self-taught typists can match trained ones. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test is a quick way to benchmark and track your progress.' },
  { q: 'Does touch typing really beat hunt-and-peck?', a: 'Usually, because looking at the keyboard limits you, and hunt-and-peck typists often plateau near 30 WPM. That said, a large study found self-taught typists using fewer than ten fingers can be as fast as strict touch typists, so technique matters more than a fixed hand position. Benchmark both ways with the Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test.' },
  { q: 'Does the keyboard layout affect speed?', a: 'Less than people expect. QWERTY dates to the 1870s, and the Dvorak layout, often claimed to be far faster, has not shown a decisive real-world advantage in government and academic studies. Layout is a small part of typing. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test focuses on technique and practice, which move your speed far more.' },
  { q: 'Why do I get different scores on different typing tests?', a: 'Because tests use different text, timing, and scoring. Common-word tests read faster than real quotes with punctuation, and some show gross WPM while others show net. Backspace handling and length also nudge the number. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test uses realistic text and net WPM with a clear formula, so the score reflects real conditions.' },
  { q: 'Which test duration is most accurate?', a: 'A 15-second test measures burst speed and swings on luck, 60 seconds is a fair general snapshot, and 3 minutes best reflects the speed you can sustain, which matters for a job. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test offers all four so you can pick a quick check or a realistic run depending on your goal.' },
  { q: 'Do I need an account to take the test or see my score?', a: 'No. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test needs no registration, login, or personal details. Open the page, pick a duration, and type. When you finish you can download a PDF of your result. Because there is no account, nothing is stored on a server, and the whole test runs in your browser.' },
  { q: 'Are my keystrokes sent to a server?', a: 'No. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test processes your keystrokes on your own device with client-side JavaScript, so nothing is transmitted. You can confirm this by opening the Network tab in your developer tools and seeing zero outgoing requests as you type. The PDF report is also generated in your browser.' },
  { q: 'How is this different from monkeytype or Typing.com?', a: 'Monkeytype is an excellent free, open-source test that also needs no account, and it is a fair peer. Typing.com is built around accounts and shows ads on its free tier. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test adds nothing to sign into, no ads, net WPM with KPH for job ads, and a downloadable PDF report.' },
];

const seo = {
  title: 'What Is a Good Typing Speed? (WPM and Accuracy)',
  description: 'What a good typing speed really is: the average is 52 WPM, why net WPM and accuracy beat raw speed, how WPM converts to KPH, and how to type faster.',
  url: '/playbooks/typing-speed-test/',
  type: 'WebPage' as const,
  datePublished: today,
  dateModified: today,
  breadcrumbs: [
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Playbooks', url: '/playbooks/' },
    { name: 'Typing Speed Test', url: '/playbooks/typing-speed-test/' },
  ],
  faq: faqs.map(f => ({ question: f.q, answer: f.a.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '').replace(/\s{2,}/g, ' ').trim() })),
};
---

