          </div>
          <a class="backnav" href="/playbooks/"><span class="bk-ico" aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Back to Playbooks</a>
        </div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Best Answer Hub Playbooks · Career</span>
        <div class="cav">Measured in your browser, no login</div>
        <h1>What Counts as a <em>Good Typing Speed</em></h1>
        <p class="dek">A plain guide to typing speed: how words per minute is actually measured, why net WPM and accuracy matter more than a headline number, what counts as a good speed against real data, how WPM converts to the keystrokes-per-hour figure job ads ask for, and how to get faster. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test measures all of it in your browser, with no signup.</p>
        <div class="byline"><span><b>Best Answer Hub</b></span><span>~9 min read</span><span>Career tools</span><span>No signup</span></div>
        <div class="bandstrip">
          <div class="seg b1">Net WPM and accuracy<small>the honest score</small></div>
          <div class="seg b2">Private<small>nothing sent</small></div>
          <div class="seg b3">Free<small>no signup, no ads</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">52 WPM</div><div class="l">average typing speed across 168,000 people</div><div class="src">Aalto, 2018</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">120 WPM</div><div class="l">the speed the fastest typists in that study reached</div><div class="src">Aalto, 2018</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">5 chars</div><div class="l">count as one word in every WPM score</div><div class="src">text-entry standard</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">0</div><div class="l">signup, ads, or keystrokes sent to a server</div><div class="src">runs in your browser</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <div class="t">On this page</div>
        <a href="#what">What it is</a>
        <a href="#measure">How WPM is measured</a>
        <a href="#good">What is a good speed</a>
        <a href="#accuracy">Why accuracy wins</a>
        <a href="#faster">How to type faster</a>
        <a href="#layouts">Does layout matter</a>
        <a href="#different">Versus other tests</a>
        <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
      </nav>

      <article>
        <p class="lead"><strong>The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test is a free, browser-based tool that measures your typing speed as net words per minute, along with your accuracy and your keystrokes per hour, then lets you download a timestamped PDF you can attach to a job application, all with no signup and nothing sent to a server.</strong> This guide explains how WPM is calculated, what counts as a good speed against real data, why accuracy matters more than raw speed, and the practical way to get faster.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/typing-speed-test/">Typing Speed Test</a> times you against a passage and reports how fast and how cleanly you type. It shows gross WPM, net WPM with an accuracy penalty applied, your accuracy percentage, and your keystrokes per hour, and it offers four durations from a 15-second burst to a 3-minute run that better reflects sustainable speed. When you finish, you can download a dated PDF report to keep or send to a hiring manager. Every calculation runs as JavaScript on your own device, so your keystrokes are never uploaded and no account is required. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/career-toolkit/">Career Toolkit</a> next to a <a href="/tools/resume-builder/">Resume Builder</a> and a <a href="/tools/word-counter/">Word Counter</a>, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

        <div class="cav">The arithmetic</div>
        <h2 id="measure">How is typing speed measured?</h2>
        <p>Typing speed is measured in words per minute, where a word is standardized to five characters, including spaces and punctuation, rather than an actual dictionary word. That convention keeps scores comparable no matter what text you type. Gross WPM is simply the characters you typed divided by five, then divided by the minutes elapsed. Net WPM is the honest figure: it subtracts your uncorrected errors before doing the same sum, so mistakes actually cost you. Accuracy is a separate percentage, the share of characters you got right. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows net WPM as your main score because it reflects real, usable output. It also converts your result into keystrokes per hour, the unit many data-entry job ads use, by multiplying net WPM by 300, since five characters a word across sixty minutes is 300. So 60 net WPM is about 18,000 keystrokes per hour.</p>

        <div class="cav">The honest benchmark</div>
        <h2 id="good">What is a good typing speed?</h2>
        <p>A good way to judge your speed is against real data, and the best measurement comes from a large study. Researchers at Aalto University and the University of Cambridge analyzed 136 million keystrokes from 168,000 volunteers and found an average typing speed of about 52 words per minute, with most people typing between 30 and 60, and the fastest reaching 120. So anything around 50 is squarely average, the 60s and 70s are genuinely quick, and 90 or above is exceptional. Job expectations sit on top of this: many office and administrative roles look for roughly 50 to 60 WPM with high accuracy, while data-entry and transcription roles often ask for more. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows where your result lands so the number means something rather than floating on its own.</p>
        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">Where typing speeds fall, from average to fastest</figcaption>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 600 300" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of typing speeds: hunt-and-peck around 30 words per minute, average 52, strong around 70, and the fastest typists in the Aalto study 120 words per minute.">
            <defs><linearGradient id="bg" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="0"><stop offset="0" stop-color="#C6603F"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#8a4429"/></linearGradient></defs>
            <text x="20" y="30" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#847B6B">Typing speed in words per minute</text>
            <text x="20" y="72" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#33302A">Hunt and peck</text>
            <rect x="20" y="82" width="75" height="30" rx="6" fill="url(#bg)"/>
            <text x="107" y="103" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">~30</text>
            <text x="20" y="132" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#33302A">Average</text>
            <rect x="20" y="142" width="130" height="30" rx="6" fill="url(#bg)"/>
            <text x="162" y="163" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">52</text>
            <text x="20" y="192" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#33302A">Strong</text>
            <rect x="20" y="202" width="175" height="30" rx="6" fill="url(#bg)"/>
            <text x="207" y="223" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">~70</text>
            <text x="20" y="252" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#33302A">Fastest in the study</text>
            <rect x="20" y="262" width="300" height="30" rx="6" fill="url(#bg)"/>
            <text x="332" y="283" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">120</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="chart-src">Average of 52 WPM and the 120 WPM fastest are from the 136-million-keystroke study (Aalto University and University of Cambridge, 2018). The hunt-and-peck and strong figures are typical benchmarks shown for context.</p>
        </figure>

        <div class="cav">Speed is not the goal</div>
        <h2 id="accuracy">Why does accuracy matter more than raw speed?</h2>
        <p>Because an error costs more than the second it took to make, so a clean 55 WPM beats a sloppy 70. Every mistake you leave in has to be found and fixed later, and net WPM captures this by subtracting uncorrected errors from your score, which is why the Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test leads with net WPM rather than the flattering gross number. Typing teachers give the same advice in reverse: build accuracy first and let speed follow. If you push for a big number at 85 percent accuracy, you are rehearsing your mistakes until they become automatic, which is far harder to unlearn than to avoid. A sensible target is 95 percent accuracy or better, and once that is steady, speed tends to rise on its own. The test shows accuracy live as you type, so you can feel the trade-off in real time rather than only at the end.</p>
        <blockquote>A fast score with errors is not fast. <span class="accent">Net WPM counts only the words you got right, which is the only speed that helps you at work.</span></blockquote>

        <div class="cav">The way up</div>
        <h2 id="faster">How do you type faster?</h2>
        <p>You get faster mostly by typing without looking and by practicing a little every day, and the research is encouraging about how. The large keystroke study found that fast typists share a habit called rollover, pressing the next key before releasing the last, in 40 to 70 percent of keystrokes, and that this holds whether or not they were formally trained. It also found that self-taught typists using fewer than ten fingers can be as fast as strict touch typists, so there is no single correct hand position you must adopt. What consistently helps is keeping your eyes on the screen rather than the keyboard, letting your fingers learn the layout, and drilling the specific letters and combinations that trip you up. Short daily sessions of 15 to 20 minutes build the muscle memory faster than occasional long ones. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test is a quick way to benchmark now and again after a few weeks to see the change.</p>

        <div class="cav">A common myth</div>
        <h2 id="layouts">Does the keyboard layout change your speed?</h2>
        <p>Far less than most people think, so QWERTY is rarely what is holding you back. The QWERTY layout was devised in the 1870s by Christopher Latham Sholes and spread with the early Remington typewriters, and it has been the default ever since. The best-known alternative, Dvorak, is often said to be dramatically faster, but the evidence does not support a decisive real-world advantage: a United States government study in 1956 found no meaningful benefit, and later economic and ergonomic reviews reached the same conclusion. Modern analysis holds that layout is only a small part of the physical act of typing, and that many layouts allow similar speeds once a typist is trained on them. The practical takeaway is that technique and practice move your speed far more than switching layouts, which is why the Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test focuses on measuring and improving how you type rather than what keys sit where.</p>

        <div class="cav">The honest comparison</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is it different from other typing tests?</h2>
        <p>The difference is a clean, gateless run: the Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test shows your result instantly with no account to create, no ads around the test, and a PDF you can attach to an application, while it keeps every keystroke in your browser. To be fair, Monkeytype is an excellent free, open-source test that also needs no account to type, and it deserves the credit. The contrast is sharper with the others: education-focused sites like Typing.com are built around student and teacher accounts and show ads on the free tier, and several tests invite you to sign in to save a score or join a leaderboard. The table sets the usual experience next to this one.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you get</th><th>Best Answer Hub</th><th>Typical typing test</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Signup to start or see your score</td><td class="band">Never</td><td>Often pushed to save a result</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ads around the test</td><td class="band">None</td><td>Common on free tiers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Account for scores or leaderboard</td><td class="band">Not needed</td><td>Common</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Score shown</td><td class="band">Net WPM, accuracy, and KPH</td><td>Varies, sometimes gross only</td></tr>
            <tr><td>PDF report for a job application</td><td class="band">Yes</td><td>Rare</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where it runs</td><td class="band">In your browser</td><td>Also in the browser, behind accounts or ads</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>None of this makes the timing special, because the WPM sum is the same everywhere. What differs is the experience around it: whether you have to sign in to see your own score, whether ads crowd the page, and whether you leave with something you can actually use. The Best Answer Hub Typing Speed Test keeps it clean and hands you a report.</p>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">Pair it with the rest of the toolkit</div><p>A typing score is one line on an application. The Best Answer Hub <a href="/career-toolkit/">Career Toolkit</a> covers the rest: a <a href="/tools/resume-builder/">Resume Builder</a> with a real PDF and Word export, a <a href="/tools/cover-letter-builder/">Cover Letter Builder</a>, and a <a href="/tools/word-counter/">Word Counter</a> for hitting a length limit, all free and all in your browser.</p></div>

        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">See your real number</span>
          <h3>Open the Typing Speed Test</h3>
          <p>Free, no signup, and measured entirely in your browser. Get your net WPM, accuracy, and keystrokes per hour, then download a dated PDF to attach to a job application.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/tools/typing-speed-test/">Test my typing speed</a>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">Good questions</div>
        <h2 id="faq">Common questions about typing speed</h2>
        <div class="faq">
