            <div class="qa">
              <div class="q" set:html={f.q}></div>
              <div class="a" set:html={f.a}></div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="cav">People also read</div>
        <h2>Keep going</h2>
        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/free-career-tools/">Free Career and Resume Tools, Nothing Uploaded</a></b> The Career Toolkit guide: resume and cover letter builders, the word counter, and the typing test.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/resume-builder/">Build an ATS-Friendly Resume, Free to Download</a></b> Turn a strong typing score into a resume that gets past the filters.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/word-counter/">Writing to a Word or Character Limit</a></b> Hit an exact length on a cover letter or application, privately.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/how-good-is-your-ai-knowledge/">How Good Is Your AI Knowledge?</a></b> A free, no-signup check on where you really stand with everyday AI tools.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li>Aalto University, <a href="https://www.aalto.fi/en/news/the-traits-of-fast-typists-discovered-by-analysing-136-million-keystrokes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The traits of fast typists, from 136 million keystrokes</a>, 2018 (with the University of Cambridge; 168,000 volunteers, average 52 WPM, most 30 to 60, fastest 120, and the rollover habit of fast typists), reporting the ACM CHI 2018 paper <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3173574.3174220" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Observations on Typing from 136 Million Keystrokes</a>.</li>
          <li>Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Words_per_minute" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Words per minute</a>, 2026 (the standard that one word equals five characters, and typical benchmark speeds).</li>
